    <main class="lesson-content">
        <article class="lesson-article">
            <header class="lesson-header"><span class="lesson-number">Lesson 13</span><h1>Saga Pattern</h1><div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>35 min</span><span class="lesson-level advanced">Advanced</span></div></header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Distributed Transactions Problem</h2>
                    <p>In microservices, you can't use ACID transactions across services. Sagas provide eventual consistency through compensating actions.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Choreography vs Orchestration</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Choreography</h4>
                            <p>Each service publishes events, others react</p>
                            <pre><code>Order → InventoryReserved → PaymentProcessed → OrderCompleted</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Orchestration</h4>
                            <p>Central coordinator manages the flow</p>
                            <pre><code>Orchestrator → Reserve → Charge → Complete</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>MassTransit Saga State Machine</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">public class OrderSaga : MassTransitStateMachine&lt;OrderSagaState&gt;
{
    public State Submitted { get; private set; }
    public State InventoryReserved { get; private set; }
    public State PaymentProcessed { get; private set; }
    
    public Event&lt;OrderSubmitted&gt; OrderSubmitted { get; private set; }
    public Event&lt;InventoryReserved&gt; InventoryReserved { get; private set; }
    public Event&lt;PaymentFailed&gt; PaymentFailed { get; private set; }

    public OrderSaga()
    {
        Initially(
            When(OrderSubmitted)
                .Then(ctx =&gt; ctx.Saga.OrderId = ctx.Message.OrderId)
                .Publish(ctx =&gt; new ReserveInventory(ctx.Message.OrderId))
                .TransitionTo(Submitted));

        During(Submitted,
            When(InventoryReserved)
                .Publish(ctx =&gt; new ProcessPayment(ctx.Saga.OrderId))
                .TransitionTo(InventoryReserved));

        During(InventoryReserved,
            When(PaymentFailed)
                .Publish(ctx =&gt; new ReleaseInventory(ctx.Saga.OrderId))  // Compensate!
                .TransitionTo(Submitted));
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Compensating Actions</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-text">Step 1: Reserve Inventory  ← Compensate: Release Inventory
Step 2: Charge Payment     ← Compensate: Refund Payment
Step 3: Ship Order         ← Compensate: Cancel Shipment

If Step 2 fails → Run Compensate for Step 1</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Sagas manage distributed transactions.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Compensating actions rollback on failure.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>MassTransit provides saga state machines.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
